WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.640
- [Narrator] Nearly $300 billion.

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:03.510
That's how much money

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:05.790
the Federal Trade
Commission estimates workers

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:07.620
could be missing out on each year

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.810
because of noncompete agreements.

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:11.790
- Interestingly, we see wages depress,

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:13.920
not just for people who
are directly subject

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:16.590
to noncompetes, but even
for workers that are not.

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:18.480
- [Narrator] A new proposal from the FTC

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:21.240
would ban noncompete clauses
at the federal level,

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.300
following states that have
already made them unenforceable.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.820
- The FTC thinks this is gonna
be really good for workers.

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:29.190
It will make companies
have to think differently,

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:30.360
maybe more creatively,

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:32.280
about how they're gonna retain people.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:33.330
- [Narrator] Here's what a federal ban

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:37.140
on noncompetes could mean
for workers and businesses,

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:40.110
and why it faces challenges.

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:43.050
Noncompetes typically
restrict workers from jumping

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:45.360
to competitors after they leave a company.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.430
For example, this is a noncompete clause

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:49.500
for Amazon from 2018.

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:52.710
It specifies an amount of
time that workers have to wait

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:54.540
before moving to a competitor.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:56.760
Others can also bar people from working

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:58.710
in a certain geographic area.

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:00.990
- Employers include these in contracts

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:04.560
because they want to protect
confidential information.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:08.100
They wanna protect trade
secrets, proprietary information.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.320
That might be everything
from a scientific formula

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.080
to a list of customers.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:16.260
- [Narrator] The FTC estimates
that almost 20% of workers

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:19.080
in the US are affected
by noncompete agreements.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:22.260
That includes people across
industries and positions,

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:25.080
from business executives
to lower wage workers,

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:29.520
who likely have less access
to proprietary information.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:31.590
Regulation for enforcing them right now

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:35.010
is entirely up to states,
but the Biden administration

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:37.350
has pushed for federal regulation.

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:39.540
- Workers should be free
to take a better job

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:40.830
if someone offers it.

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:43.470
- [Narrator] The FTC says
that noncompete clauses hurt

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:46.290
the US labor market and
that eliminating them

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:48.960
would give workers more
mobility in their industry,

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:50.880
which could increase wages.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.490
- Noncompetes mean that somebody can't go

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:55.710
to another company where
they might be offered

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:57.930
a higher salary or a higher wage.

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:00.030
It also means that they
can't go and get an offer

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:02.370
from another company and
come back to their employer

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:04.710
and say, "Hey," you
know, "here's my offer.

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:05.700
Will you meet it?"

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:08.940
It doesn't give them the means
to negotiate for higher pay.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:10.860
- [Narrator] After a ban
on noncompete clauses

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:13.830
for hourly workers in Oregon in 2008,

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:17.400
wages increased by 2 to 3% on average.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:20.520
Something similar
happened in Hawaii in 2012

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:23.580
when wages for new tech
hires went up by 4%

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:26.190
after the state introduced
a ban in the sector.

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:28.200
Proponents of a federal ban say

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:30.870
it could also promote entrepreneurship

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:33.480
by freeing employees up
to join a new business

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:34.890
or start their own.

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:37.230
- The FTC's idea is that by
giving people the freedom

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:38.940
to do that without these restrictions,

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:41.490
it will be both, you
know, good for individuals

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:43.440
and also good for the economy.

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:45.090
- [Narrator] But it's a
little more complicated

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:46.170
for companies.

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:48.960
On one hand, the potential
ban could give companies

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:52.470
a broader hiring pool and more
chances to bring on talent,

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:55.650
but opponents of the ban also
say it offers less protection

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:58.980
for trade secrets, which
aren't fully covered by laws.

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:00.907
- When I've asked lawyers
about this, they say,

00:03:00.907 --> 00:03:03.990
"Yeah, but it's tricky
because trade secrets laws

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:07.020
you can only impose after
the fact, you can't monitor

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:09.690
and track what they're
telling other people

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:10.950
or what they're using."

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:14.400
So, you know, they say
that noncompetes really are

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.160
the only way they they can
know that their information

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:18.240
is being protected.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:19.680
- [Narrator] If adopted,
companies would need

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:22.290
to rescind these clauses from any contract

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:24.960
and inform employees past and present.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:28.080
The US Chamber of Commerce says
that the FTC's proposed ban

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:29.940
is blatantly unlawful.

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:32.640
- No matter what rule
the FTC comes out with,

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:35.250
they will probably be
challenged in court about

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:37.860
the parameters or even whether
they have the authority

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:39.630
to create a rule like this.

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:41.550
- [Narrator] The FTC says it does.

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:44.190
- We are confident that
the text and instructor

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:46.890
of the FTC Act gives us
clear authority to do this.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:49.050
- [Narrator] But the proposal
still has some time before

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:50.040
it gets to that point.

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:52.920
It has to go through 60 days
of public comment first,

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:55.557
where anyone can offer feedback.

00:03:55.557 --> 00:03:58.230
Then the agency can formally
make the proposed rule

00:03:58.230 --> 00:03:59.280
a regulation.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:01.650
- At the moment, it's very
broad and it basically applies

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:02.910
to any noncompete.

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:04.440
The final rule may be different from them.

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:05.910
They might modify some of it.

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:08.660
- [Narrator] The public
comment period ends March 10th.

